Having demonstrated the photoisomerization of BPDTE in PC-PCN, we then examined SO-PCN for regulating ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation. To evaluate the ability of SO-PCN in generating singlet oxygen, 1,3-diphenylisobenzofuran (DPBF), a wellknown ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ scavenger, was used to detect the ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ produced in our system. Upon oxidative degradation of DPBF by ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ , the absorption of DPBF at $\lambda{=}410\mathrm{nm}$ decreased, thus serving as an indication of the singlet oxygen generated. First, the experiment was carried out using a visible light source ( $\lambda>$ $450\mathrm{nm}$ ) at room temperature to examine the photosensitizing ability of SO-PCN. Prior to addition of the probe, SO-PCN was washed thoroughly with hot DMF followed by acetonitrile until no residual ligands in the supernatant were detected by UV/Vis spectroscopy. Acetonitrile was bubbled with oxygen for 20 minutes before the measurements. As a control experiment, DPBF $(50\upmu\mathrm{M})$ in acetonitrile without SO-PCN was irradiated by visible light $\langle\lambda>450\mathrm{nm}\rangle$ ). Only a negligible decrease in the absorbance of DPBF was observed during 150 seconds of irradiation, and indicates DPBF was stable under these conditions. Knowing this, we examined the photosensitization of the open form of SO-PCN $(1.42\upmu\mathrm{mol})$ using DPBF $(50~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ in $2.8\mathrm{mL}$ of acetonitrile. Upon irradiation, the absorbance at $\lambda=410\mathrm{nm}$ showed a complete degradation within 150 seconds, thus suggesting excellent photosensitizing ability of SO-PCN (see Figure 4 a).
After validating reversibility in SO-PCN, the system was excited by a $\lambda{=}405\mathrm{nm}\mathrm{CW}$ laser to examine the controlled ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation by on/off switching (Figure 3 b). Evolution of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ with the open form of SO-PCN was monitored by UV/Vis spectra every 10 seconds. Over the course of 60 seconds with $6\mathrm{mW}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ of laser power, $30\%$ of DPBF absorption decreased compared to its initial absorption, whereas the closed form of SO-PCN showed an approximately $15\%$ decrease in absorbance at $\lambda{=}410\mathrm{nm}$ . A steeper slope of the open form of SO-PCN suggests the energy of $^3[\mathrm{Zn-TCPP}]^{\ast}$ was transferred to ${}^{3}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ to generate ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ , while that of the closed form was quenched by closed BPDTE, thus resulting in less efficient ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ generation (Figure 4c). When we attempted to perform a control experiment, we found that the laser irradiation led to a complete degradation of DPBF in the absence of SO-PCN within 60 seconds (see Figure S11). Presumably because of the light sensitive nature of DPBF, the laser significantly affected DPBF solution in the absence of other chromophores. To obtain a practical blank of our system, we further examined the system with DPBF and the closed form of SO-PCN. An ${\bf N}_{2}$ saturated solvent was employed to suppress the supply of oxygen so that we could examine the system solely with photodegradation of DPBF in
