the presence of closed SO-PCN (other chromophores). Interestingly in this control experiment, the rate and extent of decrease in absorption at $\lambda{=}410\mathrm{nm}$ of DPBF were very similar to previous experimental data of the closed SO-PCN with oxygen (Figure S11), and further confirms that a successful switching operation, using photoisomerization of BPDTE towards ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation in SO-PCN, was demonstrated.
In addition to the ability to generate singlet oxygen, we further sought to study potential use of SO-PCN as a heterogeneous catalyst. Catalytic performance was demonstrated by photooxidation of DHN, which is mediated by ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ and resulted in the corresponding oxidized product juglone (Figure 5a). The photooxidation was carried out with $0.036\mathrm{mmol}$ of DHN in acetonitrile with $10\%$ SO-PCN (open form) under visible light irradiation $(\lambda>$ $450\mathrm{nm}$ ). As the reaction occurred, the absorption peaks of the starting material at $\lambda=298~\mathrm{{nm}}$ and $330\mathrm{nm}$ decreased, whereas a characteristic peak of juglone around $\lambda=$
$419\mathrm{nm}$ increased. After 30 hours, a significant decrease in absorption of DHN was observed, while leaching was not seen (Figure 5b).
In summary, through a classical pillar-layer structure, we have developed two photochromic MOFs, PC-PCN and SOPCN. Photochromism has been successfully realized in PCPCN while maintaining its single crystallinity. In particular, as a solid-state material which inherently integrates a photochromic switch and photosensitizer, SO-PCN has demonstrated reversible control of ${}^{1}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ generation. Meanwhile, SOPCN shows catalytic activity towards photooxidation of DHN. Based on the photophysical properties of the chromophore dyads realized within the MOF regime, our findings can be extended to the design of materials for potential applications in photocatalysis, photoswitching, and sensing.
## Experimental Section
Synthesis of SO-PCN: $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ -TCPP $\mathrm{10~mg},$ ), $\mathrm{Zn(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ ( $35\mathrm{mg})$ in $2\mathrm{mL}$ of DMF/DEF were ultrasonically dissolved in a $4\mathrm{mL}$ Pyrex vial. The mixture was heated at $85^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a pre-heated oven for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ . After that, BPDTE $\mathrm{(9\mg)}$ and tetrafluoroboric acid $(1\upmu\mathrm{L})$ were added and the mixture was heated for $12\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling to room temperature, purple crystals were harvested. Full experimental details can be found in the Supporting Information.
